```python
from itertools import combinations

def hall_violator(adj):
    for size in range(1, len(adj) + 1):
        for S in combinations(range(len(adj)), size):
            N = set().union(*(adj[i] for i in S))
            if len(N) < len(S):
                return S
    return None
```

This checks all $2^{|A|}$ subsets, so it takes exponential time. Hall's theorem guarantees that a violator exists exactly when no matching covers $A$ — and augmenting paths decide that in polynomial time, which is why in practice one searches for the matching, and extracts a violator from the failed search when there is none.
